      "costUsd": 0.008936999999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Let's take that road trip. Your average speed is the distance so far, over the time so far. After one hour you've gone a hundred kilometres, so your average is a hundred kilometres an hour. Keep driving, and more distance pushes the average up, but more time pulls it down. Drive at a hundred and twenty, faster than your average, and the push wins: the average rises. Slow to sixty for roadworks, and the pull wins: it falls. Now hold exactly a hundred, your average, and the two cancel, so it stays flat. That's our flat tangent. Dividing the two rates only gives your speed right now, not how fast the average is changing."
    },
